{
  "abstract": "A Lean 4 / Mathlib formalization of two results on 3-colourings of graphs. (1) Kostochka and Yancey's theorem, the case k = 4 of Ore's 1967 conjecture on the minimum number of edges of k-critical graphs: every finite 4-critical graph G satisfies 3|E(G)| ≥ 5|V(G)| - 2. The proof follows their potential method (potential 5|R| - 3e(R), the auxiliary graph Y(G,R,φ), the structural claims on a minimal counterexample, and a discharging argument). (2) Grötzsch's theorem: every finite triangle-free planar graph is 3-colourable, deduced from (1) as in Kostochka–Yancey: a minimal counterexample is 4-critical; if all faces have length at least 5, Euler's formula contradicts (1); otherwise identifying opposite vertices of a 4-face keeps the graph planar and smaller, so both identifications create triangles, giving two vertex-disjoint paths between opposite corners of the face; together with an apex placed inside the face they form a K5 minor of a planar graph, a contradiction (this replaces the topological crossing argument of the paper). Planarity is defined combinatorially, by rotation systems of genus zero (Euler defect 0); as sanity checks on this definition K4 is shown planar and K5 and K3,3 non-planar.",
